The '''2026 UCI Road World Championships''' was the 99th edition of the [[UCI Road World Championships]], the annual world championships for [[road bicycle racing]]. It was held from 20 to 27 September 2026 in [[Montreal]], [[Quebec]], [[Canada]].<ref name="uci-announce">{{cite web |url=https://www.uci.org/competition-hub/2026-uci-road-world-championships/14QBdFX8GqbCHUPE234D0Q |title=2026 UCI Road World Championships |publisher=[[Union Cycliste Internationale]] |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref><ref name="montreal2026">{{cite web |url=https://www.montreal2026.org/en/ |title=Home – Montréal 2026 |publisher=Montréal 2026 Organizing Committee |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref>

The championships marked the return of the UCI Road World Championships to Montreal 52 years after the city hosted the [[1974 UCI Road World Championships|1974 edition]], which was the first time the event was held outside [[Europe]].<ref name="montreal2026"/> It was the third time Canada hosted the event, after Montreal in 1974 and [[Hamilton, Ontario|Hamilton]] in [[2003 UCI Road World Championships|2003]].<ref name="cyclingnews-routes">{{cite web |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/races/uci-road-world-championships-2026-2026/map/ |title=2026 UCI Road World Championships routes |publisher=Cyclingnews |date=27 September 2025 |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref>

A total of thirteen events were held, consisting of six road races, six [[individual time trial]]s and a [[team time trial]] mixed relay.<ref name="uci-200days">{{cite web |url=https://www.uci.org/pressrelease/200-days-to-go-a-look-at-the-courses-of-the-2026-uci-road-world/7MNCbBHZySudjNAoDW4R8x |title=200 days to go: a look at the courses of the 2026 UCI Road World Championships in Montreal |publisher=[[Union Cycliste Internationale]] |date=4 March 2026 |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref> Approximately 1,000 athletes from around 75 countries were expected to compete.<ref name="montreal2026"/>

== Background ==
The event was awarded to [[Montreal]] at the [[Union Cycliste Internationale|UCI]] Congress held during the [[2022 UCI Road World Championships]] in [[Wollongong]], [[Australia]].<ref name="montreal2026"/> The event was organized by the promoter of the [[Grand Prix Cycliste de Québec]] et [[Grand Prix Cycliste de Montréal|de Montréal]], with support from [[Cycling Canada]].<ref name="montreal2026"/>

The Canadian federal government announced a CA$13-million investment to support the hosting of the championships, with CA$9.8 million from Sport Canada through the Hosting Program and CA$3.2 million from Canada Economic Development for Quebec Regions.<ref name="gpcqm">{{cite web |url=https://gpcqm.ca/en/montreal-presentera-les-championnats-du-monde-route-uci-2026/ |title=Montréal to host 2026 UCI Road World Championships |publisher=Grands Prix Cyclistes de Québec et de Montréal |date=9 September 2023 |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref>

The event was expected to be the largest sporting event in Montreal since the [[1976 Summer Olympics]].<ref name="montreal2026"/> Admission to all events was free of charge.<ref name="faq">{{cite web |url=https://www.montreal2026.org/en/faq/ |title=FAQ – Montréal 2026 |publisher=Montréal 2026 Organizing Committee |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref>

== Course ==
The routes were unveiled during the [[2025 UCI Road World Championships]] in [[Kigali]], [[Rwanda]].<ref name="cyclingnews-routes"/>

=== Road races ===
The road races featured a final circuit of {{convert|13.4|km|mi}} around [[Mount Royal]] in downtown Montreal, with {{convert|269|m|ft}} of elevation gain per lap.<ref name="uci-200days"/> The circuit included the Voie Camillien-Houde climb ({{convert|2.3|km|mi}} at 6.2% average gradient), the Chemin de la Polytechnique with sections exceeding 11% gradient, and a false flat rising along Avenue du Parc, which served as the finish line for all thirteen events.<ref name="uci-200days"/> The circuit was similar to that used by the [[Grand Prix Cycliste de Montréal]], as well as previous cycling events in Montréal such as the [[1974 UCI Road World Championships]] and [[Cycling at the 1976 Summer Olympics – Men's individual road race|1976 Summer Olympics]].

The elite men's road race covered {{convert|273.4|km|mi}} with {{convert|3803|m|ft}} of total elevation gain,{{sfn|''Technical Guide''|2026|p=101}} while the elite women's road race spanned {{convert|180.1|km|mi}} with {{convert|2570|m|ft}} of elevation gain.{{sfn|''Technical Guide''|2026|p=91}} Both elite road races started in [[Brossard]], in the [[Montérégie]] region, before passing through several municipalities and crossing the [[Samuel-De Champlain Bridge|Samuel De Champlain Bridge]] to reach the Mount Royal circuit in downtown Montréal.<ref name="uci-200days"/><ref name="cyclingnews-routes"/>

=== Time trials ===
[[File:Amateur bikers — UCI Road World Championships Course.jpg|thumb|Amateur bikers ride the time trial course in Old Montreal on 19 September.]]
For the first time since [[2022 UCI Road World Championships|2022]], the time trial courses for elite men and women was identical in length – a {{convert|39.2|km|mi|abbr=off|adj=on}} route through Montreal, racing along the [[Saint Lawrence River]], crossing the [[St. Lawrence River]], passing through the [[Circuit Gilles Villeneuve]], and returning via the [[Pont de la Concorde (Montreal)|Concorde Bridge]].<ref name="cyclingnews-routes2">{{cite web |url=https://www.cyclingnews.com/races/uci-road-world-championships-2026-2026/map/ |title=2026 UCI Road World Championships routes |publisher=Cyclingnews |date=27 September 2025 |access-date=2026-04-11}}</ref>

=== Virtual route ===
Online indoor exercise platform and host of the 2026 UCI Cycling Esports World Championships, [[MyWhoosh]], had a virtual route replicating the Mont Royal final circuit of the road races.<ref name="uci-200days"/>

== Schedule ==
All events started and finished in Montreal, with the exception of the elite road races, which started at [[Quartier DIX30]] in [[Brossard]]. [[Park Avenue (Montreal)|Avenue du Parc]] served as the finish line for all events.<ref name="uci-200days"/>

All times listed below were for the local time – [[Time in Canada#Eastern Time Zone|Eastern Daylight Time]] or [[UTC−04:00]].

